Subject: Fuse The Argument Element Load And The For-In Branch

What this does
  Two more superinstructions, in the slots of retired opcodes.
  `get_arg(a) get_loc(l) get_array_el` becomes `OP_get_arg8_loc8_array_el`,
  which takes the receiver from the argument frame and the key from a local;
  and `for_in_next if_false(L)` becomes `OP_for_in_next_if_false(L)`, whose
  operand layout is byte-for-byte what the branch expects.

Why we need it
  Round 1 folded the local load and the element load but left the receiver
  alone, and on prop-diff code the receiver is a function parameter -- so
  `nextProps[propKey]` still paid two dispatches and a stack round trip for the
  receiver. The for-in shape is the other half of the same code: every
  `for_in_next` on that path is immediately followed by its conditional branch,
  whose sense is always the same because the parser emits `if (!done) goto body`.

What it adds
  Both handlers do their own work and then fall through into the general handler
  rather than repeating it, so neither adds a local, a live value or an exit to
  JS_CallInternal and neither costs recursion depth. The receiver in the triple
  is OWNED, not borrowed: it lives in the argument frame, which user code can
  overwrite while the key's ToPropertyKey runs, so the handler takes its own
  reference. Five opcodes are retired to pay for the two new ones -- put_arg2,
  put_arg3 and set_arg1..3, which are pure size aliases with a general form
  already -- and the three banked slots raise InternalError if executed, so a
  desynchronised stream fails loudly instead of returning wrong answers.
  The fused branch needs a case in compute_stack_size: an unlisted branch opcode
  leaves its target unvisited and mis-sizes the stack. No bytecode version bump:
  `9999` raises the version and carries the regenerated built-in blobs.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. `9999` carries the version and the rebuilt built-in blobs.

